# Cross-frame fault drop-correctness (error-model.md §11, Increment 2.1a, Q9 —
# the subtlest property): the callee `faulty`'s frame holds a LIVE Drop-bearing
# local (`g`, a Guard with a custom drop) when the overflow happens. The fault
# propagates to `main`'s catch one frame up; the callee's early-exit drops run on
# the fault path, so `g` is dropped EXACTLY ONCE. The deterministic "drop guard N"
# print proves exactly-once on BOTH the fault path AND the no-fault path. Run under
# ASan/UBSan: clean (no leak, no double-free).
struct Guard:
    int id

equip Guard with Drop:
    void drop(!self):
        print(f"drop guard {self.id}")

# `g` is live (its field feeds the multiply) when `a * b` may overflow. On the
# fault path the early-exit drops free `g` once; on the no-fault path scope-exit
# frees it once. Expression-body form (`: a * b + ...`) so the multiply is a
# faultable op directly in the callee frame.
int faulty(int a, int b, int gid):
    Guard g = Guard(gid)
    int r = a * b + g.id
    return r

void main():
    int big = 4611686018427387904       # 2^62; big*big overflows i64
    int caught = faulty(big, big, 1) catch Fault.Overflow: -1
    print(f"{caught}")                   # fault path → -1 (after "drop guard 1")
    int ok = faulty(3, 4, 2) catch Fault.Overflow: -1
    print(f"{ok}")                       # no-fault path → 3*4 + 2 = 14 (drop guard 2)
